#: app/page.tsx
msgid "A tiny face detector and tracker on the CPU pick a layout per shot cut: follow the speaker, stack two people, slides over the webcam tile, follow a moving subject when there is no face, or fit the whole frame. ffmpeg burns in captions, hook, intro and outro, fades and crossfades in one encode."
msgstr "A tiny face detector and tracker on the CPU pick a layout per shot cut: follow the speaker, stack two people, slides over the webcam tile, follow a moving subject when there is no face, or fit the whole frame. ffmpeg burns in captions, hook, intro and outro, fades and crossfades in one encode."

#: content/use-cases.ts
msgid "A Twitch clip is 16:9, at most a minute, and cut by whoever pressed the button. A TikTok is 9:16, captioned, and starts on its hook. Getting from one to the other by hand is an hour of scrubbing per stream."
msgstr "A Twitch clip is 16:9, at most a minute, and cut by whoever pressed the button. A TikTok is 9:16, captioned, and starts on its hook. Getting from one to the other by hand is an hour of scrubbing per stream."

#: content/guides.ts
msgid "A YouTube Data API v3 videos.insert body per clip (private by default) and a TikTok Content Posting API body, so a script or agent can upload."
msgstr "A YouTube Data API v3 videos.insert body per clip (private by default) and a TikTok Content Posting API body, so a script or agent can upload."

#: content/guides.ts
msgid "A YouTube link is the most common input to a clipping tool, and the first thing hosted services charge for is the minutes in it. Reelsaw takes the same link and does the whole job on your hardware: fetch, transcribe, pick moments, reframe to 9:16, caption, and hand back clips with upload metadata."
msgstr "A YouTube link is the most common input to a clipping tool, and the first thing hosted services charge for is the minutes in it. Reelsaw takes the same link and does the whole job on your hardware: fetch, transcribe, pick moments, reframe to 9:16, caption, and hand back clips with upload metadata."

