#: content/guides.ts
msgid "Language models rank what they see at the start and end of a long prompt better than what is in the middle. Reelsaw chunks the transcript at long pauses with 20% overlap, collects every standalone moment per chunk in parallel, then pools and ranks them. Weak picks are dropped relative to the run, not by a fixed count, so a two-hour episode yields as many clips as it earns."
msgstr "Language models rank what they see at the start and end of a long prompt better than what is in the middle. Reelsaw chunks the transcript at long pauses with 20% overlap, collects every standalone moment per chunk in parallel, then pools and ranks them. Weak picks are dropped relative to the run, not by a fixed count, so a two-hour episode yields as many clips as it earns."

#: content/use-cases.ts
msgid "Language models rank what they see at the start and end of a long prompt better than what sits in the middle. Reelsaw chunks the transcript at long pauses with 20% overlap, collects every standalone moment per chunk in parallel, then pools and ranks them, so a scene in hour six competes on equal terms with the opening. There is no fixed clip count: a stream yields as many clips as it earns."
msgstr "Language models rank what they see at the start and end of a long prompt better than what sits in the middle. Reelsaw chunks the transcript at long pauses with 20% overlap, collects every standalone moment per chunk in parallel, then pools and ranks them, so a scene in hour six competes on equal terms with the opening. There is no fixed clip count: a stream yields as many clips as it earns."

